            "t": "pre",
            "text": "l r. ea dies.   dies in the meaning ' a fixed day ' is nearly\nalways feminine.\n  ll. 1-2. Cotuato      et...    ducibus,        'under   the    leadership\n     l. 3. Cenabum is the modern Orléans.\n   l. 4. negotiandi causa, i., 'for the sake of trading ', i.e.\n'as traders': probably as bankers or money-lenders. They\nwere always unpopular.\n     l. 4. in his, ' among them '.\n     l 5. honestum...      Romanum,         'a     respectable     Roman\nknight'.     The latter term was given to those who ranked in\nthe state socially below the senators. They were the business\nmen in Rome and large Italian towns. In the early days of\nRome,     the ' knights' were those who           could afford to equip\nthemselves as cavalry in the Roman army.\n  ll. 8-9. maior... res, 'an incident more important and\nremarkable (than usual) háppens'.      incidit is perfect, for\nLatin idiom is more precise than English in tense-usage.\n  ll 9-ro. clamore significant, 'they (ie. the Gauls) proclaim (it) by shouting ', ive. ' they shout it abroad '.\n     l. rr. tum, ' on that occasion '.\n  ll ri-1i2. quae... essent.    Note:     (i) Cenabi, loc. case,\n'*atCenabum '. (ii) quae 4- the subj., the relative clause being\n\nadverbial   (concessive)   for quae-cum      ea,   ' although   such\n   ll. 12-13. ante . . . vigiliam, 5, ' before the first watch\nfinished ', i.e. ' before the end of the... '.\n  The Romans divided the night into four ' watches ' which\nnaturally varied in length according to the time of the year.\nAs it was   roughly,   mid-winter,    the first watch   would    end\nsometime after 8 p.m."
