            "t": "pre",
            "text": "l r. appropinquassent, —appropinquavissent. So also consuessent l. 5-—consuevissent.     Verb forms containing the\nletter ' v' often drop that consonant and the following vowel.\n  l. 2-3. consumpto pabulo.       Translate as a finite verb in\nthe active, ' had used up his forage (and) '.\n   l]l. 4. expeditis. For the help given by these light-armed\ntroops to the Gallic cavalry, see Ch. 8o, ll. 7-10.\n  l. 5. insidiarum causa, ' for the sake of an ambush ', —' to\nprepare an ambush '.\n   l 5. profectum. Supply esse to give the perfect infinitive\nof this deponent verb.                     ;\n   l 5.eo...quo, 'thither . . . whither ', — 'to that place\n... Where '.\n  l. 6. pabulatum, the acc. of the supine expressing purpose.\n  l. 8. illi, as often —' the enemy '.\n   . I2. sarcinas, the soldiers' * personal gear ' or ' packs ', of\nwhich they were relieved when fighting was in prospect.\n  l, 13, expediri, ' to be prepared for action '."
