            "t": "pre",
            "text": "l r. eollis.... Thehillis the locus editus atque apertus of\n    l 3-5. hoc se colle continebant,          'kept themselves\n(— were stationed) on this hill'. fiducia  loci, ' with confidence\nof (2in) their position (hoc colle)   '.\n   l 4. interruptis pontibus, 'after breaking up the causeways '.\n   l. 5. in civitates seems merely to repeat generatim and may\ntherefore be omitted in translation. It is possible that it may\nhave been put in the margin by a copyist as an explanation\nof generatim and later been transferred mistakenly by later\ncopyists into the text itself.\n  ll. 6-8. sic... animo parati ut premerent. /;7., 'so prepared\nin their resolve that they overwhelmed,' —'fully resolved to\noverwhelm '.\n  l. 8. haesitantes   is in agreement with the        unexpressed\nobject of premerent, ' them ', i.e. the Romans:        Zi£., '(them)\nsticking fast ' «' them as they stuck fast '.\n   ]. 8-10. ut qui . . . existimaret, ' so that (a man) who saw\n(2anyone who saw) the proximity of their position would\nthink (them) ready to fight on almost equal terms '.\n   Note: (i) videret, existimaret. The subjunctives are conditional. (ii) aequo Marte.\" The Romans often used especially\nin poetry the name of a deity for the activity or thing especially associated with him or her. Here, Mars, 'the god of\nwar',—' war','fighting '. So Bacchus, —' wine '.\n   ]l ro-1i2. qui. . . cognosceret. This sentence is exactly\nparallel to the preceding one and should be translated accordingly. Insert ' but ' or ! while ' to get the contrast which\nLatin obtains merely by apposition of words, phrases, or\nclauses.   For the asyndeton, cf. also Ch. 6, 1. 8.\n  ll rr-12. inani . . . cognosceret, * would realise that they\nwere parading themselves in empty show '.\n  In thus sneering at the Gauls, Caesar seems to be deliberately trying to mislead us here; for it is obvious that Vercingetorix was hoping to entice Caesar to attack him as his\nspeech in the next chapter shows. The whole manoeuvre was\na trap and it is not therefore very convincing for Caesar to\nsay that the Gauls offered battle only because they knew\nthat the offer would not be accepted and thus could be\nregarded as ' mere empty show '.\n   ll 1i2-14. indignantes... edocet.... The skeleton of this\nsentence is Caesar edocet milites. \"The object milites has\nindignantes and      exposcentes in agreement;     dependent\nupon the former of the present participles is quod . . . interiecto. It might be better then to say: as the soldiers\nwere resentful that . . . and demanded the signal for battle,\n  l. 13-14. tantulo spatio interiecto. 'so little space lying\nbetween ', i.e. ' at so short a distance '.\n  ll r4-16. quanto ... victoriam, ' how great a loss and the\ndeath of how many brave men it was necessary that victory\ncost ', ie. ' what a terrible loss, the death of so many     brave\nmen victory must cost'.        constare, ' to cost ' is often found\nwith an abl. of price.\n  l. r6—19.   quos    . . . cariorem : the construction     is still\ndependent upon edocet. quos—eos. cum -' when '.\n   l. 18. summae iniquitatis, genitive of the matter charged,\n' for the greatest injustice '.\n   ll. 18-19. nisi... cariorem, ' unless he regarded their lives\ndearer than his own well-being '."
