            "text": "l. r. Avarici, locative case.\n    ]:*3: rex,\" after..\n    ll. 4-9. iam . . . subveniat.     Note the structure of this\n\nperiod : abl. absol, cum clause with two verbs, subordinate\nclause, main clause with dependentindirectcommand. —Translate literally and then try this suggestion: make the abl.\nabsol. a main verb, insert ' and ' before cum which should be\ndropped and then finally put * when ' before legati principes.\n  l. 6. sive, ' (to see) whether '.\n  l. 8. oratum, supine in the acc. case to express purpose.\n  ll. 8-9. maxime . . . tempore, ' at a most critical time '.\n   ll. 9-23. summo esse.... From here until the end of the\nchapter, we have indirect speech (Oratio Obliqua). See the\n  l. 1o. rem, ' the situation ', subject of esse.\n  l. 1o. quod, ' because '. cum, ' although '.\n  l. 1o. singuli ' one at a time '.\n  l. 1o. antiquitus, adverb, ' of old '.\n  l. 11. annum, acc. of duration of time, ' for one year '.\n  l. 11-2.   consuessent, —consuevissent.!\n  ll. 12-13: duo...     dicat, ' two (people) were holding office\nand each of them said he had been elected by the laws\n(zlegally) '.\n  l. 15. antiquissima . . . natum, /i/., ' born from a very old\nfamily ', ie. 'scion of... '. familia, abl. of origin.\n  ll. 16-17. magnae cognationis, ' of high connections '.\n  l. 17. proximo, ' previous '.\n  l. 20. suas... clientelas, /;7., ' of each of them (there were)\nhis own retainers', ie. 'each was supported            by his own\nretainers '. See the Introduction p. xxii.\n   l. 20. quod si, ' but if '.\n  ll. 21-2. fore uti. . . confligat, /;4., ' it would be that one\nhalf came to blows with the other half of the tribe ', i.e. ' one\nhalf would come...          '.\n\n   ! This replaces consueverant of direct speech: hence the pluperfect subj., although in every other case Caesar uses primary tenses.\n\n  fore ut 4 present or imperfect subjunctive, is an alternative\nform for the future infin. active or passive.\n   l. 22. id ne accidat, ' that it should not happen ' is the subject of positum (esse) which means ' depended '. Translate\nthe ne clause by:    ' Its prevention '."
