            "text": "l. 1. ad extremam orationem, ' at the end of his speech *.\n  ll. 3-4. neu... tribuerent, ' nor to attribute to the courage\nof the enemy what (id quod) the unfavourable ground had\nbrought about '.\n   ll. 4-5. eadem .. . quae, acc. pl. neuter, ' the same as '.\n   l. 4. cogitans, J;7., ' thinking ', is equivalent to a concessive\nclause, ' though he thought '.\n   1. 8. levi... secundo, ' after a cavalry skirmish and that\ntoo a successful one '.\n  ll ro-ir.    satis . . . existimans,   ' thinking   that sufficient\nhad been done (satis factum (esse), — he had done enough), for\nthe cockiness of the Gauls to-be-reduced and the spirit of\nhis men to-be-restored ', i.e. ' to reduce... and to restore... \".\nobject, for which see the end of Ch. 43 and the beginning of\nCh. 44, and can, therefore, withdraw from Gergovia without\ntoo much loss of face or prestige.\n   l. 12. in Aeduos —in fines Aeduorum.\n   ll. 12-13. ne . . . hostibus. Make this abl. absol. a main\nverb and insert ' and ' before tertio die.\n  l 13. ad, 'at'or' by.\n  l. 13. pontes reficit. They had been broken down by Vercingetorix.\n  l 14. eo, 'over      it'.      eo, liL, 'by means         of it' is an\nablative of the instrument."
