            "text": "ll. 1-2. prius quam . . . perficiantur, ' before the entrenchments could be completed '.\n  Temporal clauses with the secondary element of purpose\nor anticipation have their verbs in the subjunctive mood.\n   ll. 2-3. consilium capit dimittere. See the note on Ch. 26,\n   l. 4. omnes, acc. pl., object of cogant. The latter is plural\nbecause Caesar wishes to emphasise the combined efforts of\nall, not their individual role as in the verb quisque adeat.\n  ll. 6-7. ut... habeant, ' to have regard for his safety '.\n  ll. 7-8. neu...      dedant, ' and not to surrender to the foe\nfor torture one who had done such great service for the common liberty'.\n   ll. dee se...meritum, /;., ' him having deserved best for\nthe.\n  l. 9. iuo Si, ' but if '.\n  ll. 9-10.   milia.   . . LXXX,   i4,   * 80 chosen   thousands   of\nmen ', i.e. * 80,000 chosen men'. Many commentators believe\nthat Caesar has deliberately exaggerated the number.          All\nwe know, for certain, is that Alesia was large enough to contain so many.\n   l. ro. interitura. Supply esse to give the future infinitive\nactive, ' were destined to perish '.\n   l. 11. ratione inita, if, ' a calculation having been entered\nupon ', «' after a calculation had been made '.\n  l. r3. parcendo, Zi/., ' by sparing ', i.e. ' by economy '.\n  l. 16. capitis poenam, ' penalty of death '.\n  ll. 20-1. his rationibus, ' by these measures *."
